(vector (map (:db/id -1),
  (:adr/id "adr-2607181800-cloud-itonami-energy-systemdynamics-estimate"),
  (:adr/title "ADR-2607181800: cloud-itonami world-electricity coverage -- system dynamics estimate methodology"),
  (:adr/topic "cloud-itonami-energy-systemdynamics-estimate"),
  (:adr/status "accepted"),
  (:adr/date "2026-07-18"),
  (:adr/last_verified "2026-07-18"),
  (:adr/deciders (vector "Jun Kawasaki (agent-executed, standing authorization)")),
  (:adr/related (vector "adr-2607081400-cloud-itonami-energy-3512-coverage")),
  (:adr/authoritative true),
  (:adr/authoritative_for (vector "cloud-itonami energy-vertical (ISIC 3510/3511/3512) world-demand-coverage estimate methodology" "system dynamics model design used for this estimate")),
  (:adr/doc_type "adr"),
  (:adr/source-format "edn"),
  (:adr/body "# ADR-2607181800: cloud-itonami world-electricity coverage -- system dynamics estimate methodology\n\n**Status**: accepted\n**Date**: 2026-07-18\n**Deciders**: Jun Kawasaki (agent-executed, standing authorization)\n**Related**: ADR-2607081400 (cloud-itonami-isic-3512 community renewables coverage)\n\n## Context\n\nOwner asked what share of world energy demand cloud-itonami's current\npredicted production could cover. A prior repo-wide search (this\nsession, not re-run here) confirmed cloud-itonami has **no real\ngeneration assets and no measured production/coverage data anywhere in\nthe repo** -- ISIC 3510/3511/3512/3520/3530 exist only as\nbusiness-process-governance actor implementations (metering,\ndispatch/settlement, compliance audit via langgraph-clj StateGraph +\nGovernor + append-only ledger), not as power plants or a production\nforecast. The owner then asked for a system-dynamics-based estimate\nrather than a spreadsheet extrapolation. This ADR records the modeling\ndecision and where the artifacts live; it does NOT assert real business\ndata -- every cloud-itonami-specific input is a stated assumption.\n\n## Decision\n\nBuild a deterministic nbb/cljs system dynamics model\n(`90-docs/business/cloud-itonami-energy-systemdynamics-model.cljs`)\nstructured as:\n\n1. **World electricity demand baseline** -- real, cited public data\n   (Ember *Global Electricity Review 2025*: 2024 demand crossed 30,000\n   TWh, +4.0% y/y; IEA *Electricity 2025*: +3,500 TWh forecast\n   2025-2027; IEA *Energy and AI*: data-center demand doubling to ~945\n   TWh by 2030; Energy Institute *Statistical Review of World Energy\n   2025*: 592 EJ total world primary energy 2024, electricity ~18% of\n   that). Growth rate modeled as reverting from the near-term\n   AI/data-center-elevated rate (3.8%/yr) toward the 2010-2023\n   long-run historical rate (2.6%/yr) with an 8-year time constant.\n2. **Three electricity-relevant ISIC segments only** (3510 T&D grid\n   ops, 3511 SMR generation ops, 3512 community renewables ops) --\n   3520 (gas) / 3530 (steam) excluded, different unit basis, would\n   break the TWh-electricity comparison. Each segment is an\n   independent **Bass diffusion** process (innovation coefficient `p`,\n   imitation coefficient `q`, annual churn) against an assumed\n   addressable-market fraction (TAM %) of world demand -- 15% / 3% /\n   10% respectively, all explicitly stated assumptions (large\n   vertically-integrated utilities unlikely to adopt a 3rd-party\n   AI-governed ops layer soon; municipal/co-op/merchant-IPP/new-SMR\n   segment is the realistic buyer).\n3. **Onboarding-capacity stock** -- the CertGovernor human-approval-\n   in-the-loop pipeline's own throughput (TWh/yr of new managed volume\n   onboardable per year) is modeled as a separate, slower-growing\n   stock (starts at 5 TWh/yr in 2026), not an infinite tap. This is\n   the deliberate SD-modeling choice per the owner's request for an\n   *optimal* dynamics, not a naive fixed-% extrapolation.\n4. **Three policies compared** over a 2026-2051 (25-year) horizon:\n   Aggressive (uncapped bookings against Bass-desired flow, 35%/yr\n   capacity growth), Capacity-matched (bookings hard-capped at current\n   onboarding capacity, 35%/yr capacity growth), Conservative (same\n   hard cap, 15%/yr capacity growth) -- the classic SD \"Limits to\n   Growth / growth-and-underinvestment\" archetype comparison.\n\n### Why Aggressive is reported but rejected as the \"optimal\" policy\n\nAggressive produces the highest raw modeled cumulative revenue ($5.03B\nvs Capacity-matched's $2.50B over 25 years) but is **not operationally\ncredible**: uncapped Bass diffusion books ~150 TWh/yr of new managed\nthroughput in year one alone (more than most G20 countries' annual\nconsumption) from an actor with zero prior customers and a governance\npipeline sized for 5 TWh/yr. The model's only penalty for this (an\nelevated churn multiplier) understates the real consequence, which in\nreality is closer to outright delivery failure or regulatory rejection.\nIts higher modeled revenue is an artifact of ignoring the capacity\nconstraint the owner explicitly asked to be respected (\"realistic\nconstraints\" was the selected optimization criterion), not a genuine\nfinding -- so it is retained in the output as a labeled cautionary\ncounter-example, not adopted as the recommended policy.\n\n**Capacity-matched is adopted as \"optimal\"**: among the two\noperationally-credible policies (both hard-capped at onboarding\ncapacity, differing only in how fast that capacity is grown),\nCapacity-matched's faster capacity-growth investment (35%/yr vs\nConservative's 15%/yr) compounds to ~9x more cumulative revenue over\n25 years for the same governance discipline. The decision-relevant\nlesson this model produces: **the lever that matters is investment in\nonboarding/governance throughput, not sales aggressiveness against\nmarket demand.**\n\n## Headline result (Capacity-matched policy, world-electricity-demand coverage)\n\n| Year | Managed throughput (TWh/yr) | % of world electricity demand | % of world all-energy demand |\n|---|---:|---:|---:|\n| 2026 | 5 | 0.015% | 0.003% |\n| 2031 | 67 | 0.18% | 0.032% |\n| 2036 | 335 | 0.76% | 0.14% |\n| 2041 | 1,531 | 3.02% | 0.54% |\n| 2046 | 5,135 | 8.87% | 1.60% |\n| 2051 | 9,256 | 14.02% | 2.52% |\n\ncloud-itonami does not reach coverage of \"all world energy demand\"\nwithin this 25-year horizon under realistic constraints; full detail,\ncitations, and every stated assumption are in\n`90-docs/business/cloud-itonami-energy-systemdynamics-estimate.edn`.\n\n## Consequences\n\n(+) A reproducible, re-runnable estimate exists\n(`nbb 90-docs/business/cloud-itonami-energy-systemdynamics-model.cljs`\nregenerates `cloud-itonami-energy-systemdynamics-results.edn` and every\nnumber in the companion .md deterministically) rather than a one-off\nhand-computed figure that can't be checked or revised.\n\n(+) The model structure (Bass diffusion segment + capacity-constrained\nonboarding stock + policy comparison) is a reusable pattern for other\ncloud-itonami vertical-growth estimates, not specific to energy.\n\n(-) Every cloud-itonami-specific parameter (TAM %, p/q, churn, fee\nrates, capacity growth rate) is a judgment-call assumption with no\nreal data to calibrate against -- this is explicitly an illustrative\nestimate, not a forecast to be cited as cloud-itonami business\nperformance. The companion .md's §5 (Known limitations) must be read\nbefore this number is used anywhere else.\n\n(-) An interactive dashboard version (same equations, adjustable\nassumptions via sliders) was published as a Claude artifact in the\nrequesting chat session; it is not committed to this repo (ephemeral\nreport deliverable, not a monorepo app -- does not fall under the\nkotoba-uiux / kami-engine runtime-priority rules, which govern apps\ncommitted to this superproject).\n\n## Verification\n\n- `nbb 90-docs/business/cloud-itonami-energy-systemdynamics-model.cljs`\n  run successfully, stdout matches the tables in the companion .md and\n  in this ADR exactly; `cloud-itonami-energy-systemdynamics-results.edn`\n  written with the full 2026-2051 time series for all three policies.\n- World-demand baseline figures cross-checked against three\n  independent public sources (Ember, IEA x2, Energy Institute) rather\n  than a single source.\n")))
